<!--
  Cosora buyer-side route map, from the review of 2026-11-18 (main 2b02358, www.cosora.in).

  NOT the search-engine sitemap. That one is served at https://www.cosora.in/sitemap.xml by api/sitemap.ts
  (a vercel.json rewrite) and lists only public, indexable pages. This file is a working map for people:
  every buyer and shared route the app answers, what each one reads and writes in the database, and how its
  Back controls behave. It uses the sitemaps.org format so tools can read it, plus a cosora: namespace for the
  review data. Search engines must not be pointed at it.

  How each route was checked:
    - Every route was opened on production, signed out, in a 390 px wide browser. View and impression RPCs were
      answered in the browser, so no seller's numbers moved.
    - Two Back checks per route:
        cosora:back-direct  = the page's own Back control when the page was opened directly (no in-app history);
        cosora:back-browser = the browser's Back after arriving from /home/new-arrivals.
      "LEAVES SITE" means the control went to about:blank, so the visitor left Cosora.
    - DB objects come from the network log of that visit, plus a code trace for signed-in paths, plus a
      production catalog check (all 63 RPCs and 46 tables exist).
    - Migrations name the file that creates an object and the latest one that redefines it. "baseline" means a
      table older than the first migration file, which no file creates.
    - Graphify confirmed the page → hook → query edges. It has no table or migration nodes, so the DB mapping is
      from grep and the production catalog.

  Dynamic routes use one real example id, or a {placeholder} where no public example exists.
  Finding ids (BH-*, BA-*, …) refer to the review report given in chat on 2026-11-18.
-->
